content.thinking:
Note: jsdom doesn't implement ResizeObserver → `'ResizeObserver' in window` is false → no RO gets created, and `s.dom.ro` is undefined → destroy's `ro?.disconnect()` is a no-op. In other words, the "wrap subtree is collectible after destroy" check here ends up testing the bindDocument listener, not the RO (RO is already covered by unit tests in an environment where a fake RO exists… actually, the unit test only asserts the disconnected flag, it doesn't assert collectability).

To make the destroy check meaningful with respect to RO, should I also add a fake ResizeObserver that holds a strong reference like a real controller would? A real RO controller retains the observer globally. A more faithful simulation: a global registry (like an array) that holds all constructed observers — mimicking a browser global controller. Then the wrap would only become collectible if disconnect() was called (in the real browser, disconnect releases it from the controller). Hmm, this is modeling the browser, and my fake holds onto it even after disconnect unless I remove it from the registry on disconnect — which is exactly the browser's semantics (disconnecting releases the reference).

Let's model it:
```js
class FakeRO {
  static registry = [];
  constructor(cb) { this.cb = cb; FakeRO.registry.push(this); }
  observe(el) { this.el = el; }
  disconnect() { this.cb = null; this.el = null; const i = FakeRO.registry.indexOf(this); if (i>=0) FakeRO.registry.splice(i,1); }
}
window.ResizeObserver = FakeRO;
```
This mimics a global controller. If the bug existed (never disconnected), the wrap would be retained via registry → observer → cb closure → elements → wrap. After the fix, destroy disconnects → the registry is released → the wrap becomes collectible. This is a genuine pre-fix-failing / post-fix-passing proof for the destroy leak.

Let's update the script: add FakeRO before the import (the module doesn't reference RO at import time; it checks at call time — so it's fine to set it up before Chessground()).

